<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ghalib ibn Awad al-Qu'aiti</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">b. 1948</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Ghalib II al-Qu'aiti</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ghalib ibn Awad al-Qu'aiti was the last sultan of the Qu'aiti Sultanate of Hadhramaut, succeeding his father in 1966. While he was abroad in September 1967, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-liberation-front-south-yemen">National Liberation Front</a> seized <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mukalla">Mukalla</a>, and the sultanate was abolished as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-yemen">South Yemen</a> became independent. He later lived in Saudi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a> and wrote on Islamic history.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ghalib-ibn-awad-al-quaiti.jpg" alt="Ghalib ibn Awad al-Qu'aiti" width="320" height="463" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown authorUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ghalib_II_of_Hadhramaut.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
